WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:10.151
Priya Raman: Morning, both. How's Boise treating you? I saw the temperature dropped like twenty degrees overnight.

2
00:00:10.484 --> 00:00:18.343
Heather Collins: Oh it's freezing. My daughter had a soccer game at eight this morning and I was in a parka. In September.

3
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:25.067
Rosa Jimenez: Same over here in Hillcrest. The patients are all walking in with their winter coats already.

4
00:00:25.393 --> 00:00:35.877
Priya Raman: That's rough. Okay, well, I don't want to keep you too long. I mostly wanted to check in on how things are going, see if anything's come up since we last talked.

5
00:00:36.544 --> 00:00:45.042
Heather Collins: Things are good. Busy, but good. We picked up two more practices in August, I mean, so we're at nineteen now.

6
00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:47.875
Heather Collins: Still getting everybody onto the same systems.

7
00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:52.995
Priya Raman: Nineteen. Like, Nice. How's the call volume looking across the group?

8
00:00:53.665 --> 00:01:00.532
Rosa Jimenez: For Hillcrest we're averaging about six hundred a month now. Up from maybe four-fifty in the spring.

9
00:01:01.468 --> 00:01:10.400
Heather Collins: We're seeing the same trend group-wide. New patient calls are up, which is great, but it means the front desk is slammed.

10
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.430
Priya Raman: And that's mostly Google Ads driving those?

11
00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:27.210
Heather Collins: Yeah. New patient acquisition is pretty much all paid search at this point. We've got the offline conversions flowing back so the bidding is pointed at the right calls.

12
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:37.592
Priya Raman: Good. Any issues with the conversion matching at all? I know when you add locations that can sometimes take a bit to settle.

13
00:01:38.643 --> 00:01:44.700
Heather Collins: No, that's been fine. Honestly it's been quiet on the platform side, which is a nice change.

14
00:01:45.096 --> 00:01:48.434
Rosa Jimenez: Yeah, no complaints from my team. Actually, one thing...

15
00:01:49.518 --> 00:01:50.351
Priya Raman: Go ahead.

16
00:01:51.801 --> 00:02:11.657
Rosa Jimenez: So my dispatch folks, they you know, don't listen to calls. They never have time. But they've started reading the summary instead of listening to the first minute of every call, and they say it saves them a ton of time on the callbacks and the handoffs between front desk and the treatment coordinators.

17
00:02:12.559 --> 00:02:17.501
Priya Raman: Oh that's good to hear. So they're pulling the summary before they return a call?

18
00:02:18.173 --> 00:02:28.763
Rosa Jimenez: Exactly. Somebody calls in, we miss it, they call back, and instead of sitting there listening they just read what the patient said and what they wanted.

19
00:02:28.763 --> 00:02:29.548
Rosa Jimenez: Way faster.

20
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:32.310
Heather Collins: I didn't know they were doing that.

21
00:02:33.761 --> 00:02:38.934
Rosa Jimenez: I told them to try it back in July. Now they won't go back.

22
00:02:39.197 --> 00:02:47.016
Priya Raman: That's really helpful to know. Is it the practice managers mostly, uh, or is it the coordinators too?

23
00:02:47.243 --> 00:02:54.957
Rosa Jimenez: Both, honestly. The managers read them every morning anyway as part of their routine, uh, but the dispatch team is the newer one.

24
00:02:56.254 --> 00:03:02.247
Priya Raman: Okay. I'll pass that along, it's useful for us to hear where the summaries are landing.

25
00:03:02.668 --> 00:03:11.631
Heather Collins: Speaking of routine, are we still on for the quarterly review in October? I've got it on my calendar for the fifteenth.

26
00:03:12.853 --> 00:03:18.351
Priya Raman: Yes, the fifteenth works. I'll send the agenda the week before. Anything specific you want covered?

27
00:03:19.008 --> 00:03:27.048
Heather Collins: Maybe just a look at how the newer practices are doing on call handling. Two of them are still kind of finding their feet.

28
00:03:27.997 --> 00:03:30.552
Priya Raman: Happy to pull that together. Which two?

29
00:03:31.462 --> 00:03:40.535
Heather Collins: The Meridian and the Eagle locations. Both came over from a smaller group, so their front desk workflows are a little different.

30
00:03:40.763 --> 00:03:45.153
Priya Raman: Got it. I can look at their call patterns before we meet.

31
00:03:46.087 --> 00:03:56.635
Rosa Jimenez: Also, small thing, we've got a new front desk lead starting at Hillcrest next month, so I might need to get her set up with a login.

32
00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:57.807
Rosa Jimenez: I'll email you.

33
00:03:59.088 --> 00:04:04.125
Priya Raman: Yeah just send me her name and email and I'll get that going.

34
00:04:04.906 --> 00:04:05.706
Rosa Jimenez: Will do.

35
00:04:06.601 --> 00:04:12.191
Priya Raman: Okay, anything else on your end? Staffing, volume, anything that's been a headache?

36
00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:22.296
Heather Collins: The headache is just staffing in general. We're short a hygienist at the Boise location and it's making the schedule a mess.

37
00:04:22.998 --> 00:04:26.687
Rosa Jimenez: We're all feeling that. It's the same um, story everywhere.

38
00:04:27.813 --> 00:04:37.594
Priya Raman: Yeah, I hear that a lot from the dental groups. Well, on the platform side everything you know, sounds quiet, which I'll take.

39
00:04:38.831 --> 00:04:40.401
Heather Collins: Yeah, quiet is good.

40
00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:48.257
Priya Raman: Alright. I'll set up the October fifteenth review and send the agenda. Rosa, just shoot me the new hire info whenever.

41
00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:50.649
Rosa Jimenez: Will uh, do. Thanks Priya.

42
00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:52.590
Heather Collins: Thanks. Stay warm.

43
00:04:53.569 --> 00:04:55.109
Priya Raman: You too. Talk soon.
